ER MODEL DEFINITION:
The Entity–Relationship (ER) model represents the final outcome of the Purpose Formalization phase and is derived from the defined scenarios, personas, and Competency Questions.
The model is centred on two main entities: Activity and Location, which are classified as common entities. Activity represents the main entry point of user queries, while Location represents the spatial context in which activities and services take place. The relationship between Activity and Location explicitly models where an activity occurs, avoiding redundancy in the definition of properties.
Contextual entities such as SkiSlope, HikingTrail, CycleRoute, and ClimbingRoute are connected to Activity and contain activity-specific properties such as difficulty, length, elevation gain, or grade. This design allows the representation of different types of activities while keeping the core of the model simple and reusable.
The Location entity is linked to Coordinate, which represents geographic information using latitude and longitude. This supports spatial queries and ensures compatibility with OpenStreetMap-based data sources.
In addition, Hotel, Restaurant, SkiRental, and Building are connected to Location as core entities. These entities represent services that support user needs related to accommodation, food, and equipment rental, and are shared across multiple scenarios and activities.
Finally, the ViewPoint entity is included as a contextual entity associated with specific activities, providing additional points of interest and enhancing the user experience without affecting the central structure of the model.
This ER model provides a clear, coherent, and user-oriented structure that fully aligns with the identified entities and properties and serves as a solid foundation for the subsequent phases of the project.

